# Line endings are load-bearing here.
#
# klm's hardest rule is that parsing a KiCad file and re-serialising it returns
# byte-identical text, and several tests assert exactly that against fixtures in
# the repository. Git's default on Windows is `core.autocrlf=true` — including
# on GitHub's Windows runners — which rewrites every checked-out `\n` to `\r\n`.
# The fixtures would then differ from what the writer emits, and the round-trip
# tests would fail on Windows and nowhere else.
#
# So: LF in the working tree on every platform, whatever the local git config
# says. This is not a style preference; it is the same decision as the canonical
# writer's fixed float precision.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never inspects or rewrites them.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.step binary
*.pdf binary
